course: everyday
lesson: 34
title: Fire at home
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: A fire in a home grows very fast: in a few minutes a whole room can fill with thick, hot, poisonous smoke. You have very little time, so the plan is simple: get out, stay out and call for help.
explain: Smoke is the biggest danger. It is hot and lighter than the cool air, so it rises and gathers at the ceiling first. The cleanest air is near the floor, which is why you get low and crawl under the smoke.
explain: A smoke alarm smells the smoke before you do and wakes you, even at night. Homes need one on every level and near the bedrooms, tested every month.
explain: For example, if a girl wakes to the alarm and her bedroom door feels hot, the fire may be right behind it. She keeps it shut and uses her second way out, such as a window, then goes to the family's meeting place outside.
explain: If your clothes catch fire, running only feeds the flames with air. Instead you stop, drop to the ground, cover your face with your hands and roll until the fire is out.
explain: A boy in Michigan ran back into his burning house to get his video game; the smoke made him fall down, and firefighters had to carry him out.
explain: A mum in Leeds threw water on a pan of burning oil; the water turned to steam and blew flaming oil across the kitchen, and she was burnt on the arms.
fact: A small fire can fill a home with smoke in just a few minutes.
fact: Most people who die in house fires are killed by smoke, not by the flames.
fact: Hot smoke rises, so the cleanest air in a smoky room is near the floor.
fact: Smoke alarms should be on every level of a home and tested every month.
fact: Before opening a door in a fire, you feel it with the back of your hand; if it is hot, you use another way out. The back of the hand feels heat well, and your palms stay safe for crawling.
fact: Every family should have an escape plan with two ways out of each room and a meeting place outside, where everyone can be counted so nobody goes back in to look for someone.
fact: Once you are out of a burning building, you never go back inside, not even for a pet or a toy.
fact: You call the emergency number from outside: 911 in the USA and Canada, 999 in the UK and Ireland, 000 in Australia and 111 in New Zealand.
fact: If your clothes catch fire, you stop, drop, cover your face and roll; rolling smothers the flames, putting them out by cutting off their air.
fact: You should never hide under a bed or in a cupboard during a fire, because firefighters may not find you.
fact: Closing doors behind you slows the spread of fire and smoke.
fact: Water on burning oil makes the fire explode outwards.
exercise: What is the main rule when there is a fire at home? | Get out, stay out and call for help | Hide somewhere safe and wait for help | Collect your favourite things first | Try to put out the fire all by yourself
exercise: Why should you crawl low in a smoky room? | Hot smoke rises, so the cleaner air is near the floor | Fire cannot reach things that are low down | Crawling makes you move faster than walking upright through the house | Smoke is heavy and stays near the floor
exercise: What kills most people in house fires? | Breathing in smoke | Touching the flames | Falling heavy furniture | Loud noise from alarms
exercise: How can you tell if there may be fire behind a closed door? | Feel the door with the back of your hand for heat | Open it a little and look inside | Knock on it hard and listen carefully for an answer | Look at the door handle colour
exercise: Why should you use the back of your hand to feel a door? | It feels heat well and you still need your palms to crawl | The back of the hand cannot be burned at all | Palms are too cold to feel any heat | The back of the hand is tougher than the palm
exercise: Your dog is still inside the burning house. What should you do? | Tell the firefighters, but never go back in | Run back in quickly to get the dog before it's too late | Call the dog from the doorway inside | Go back in only if the smoke is thin
exercise: What does stop, drop and roll do when your clothes catch fire? | It smothers the flames by cutting off their air | It makes the flames spread more slowly to others | It cools you down by moving the air | It helps you get to the door more quickly
exercise: Why should you never hide under a bed during a fire? | Firefighters may not find you and smoke still reaches you | Beds are the first thing to burn in a house | The bed blocks the smoke alarm from working, so nobody gets woken | It makes the fire burn your room faster
exercise: Why do families choose a meeting place outside? | So everyone can be counted and nobody goes back in | So they can all watch the fire safely from close by together | So the firefighters know where to park | So the neighbours can bring blankets
exercise: How often should smoke alarms be tested? | Every month | Once every ten years | Only when a fire starts | Only when the battery is new
exercise: A boy in Dublin is outside after escaping a fire. Which number should be called? | 999 | 911 | 000 | 111
exercise: Why does closing doors behind you help in a fire? | It slows the fire and smoke from spreading | It locks the fire inside one room forever | It makes the fire burn out at once | It keeps the firefighters from getting lost
exercise: Why are smoke alarms so important at night? | They wake you when you cannot smell the smoke while asleep | They put out small fires with a spray of water from the ceiling | They call the fire brigade by themselves | They stop smoke from coming into bedrooms
exercise: Why should a home escape plan have two ways out of every room? | One way might be blocked by fire or smoke | Firefighters need two doors to come in | It makes the house cooler in summer | One door is only for adults to use, the other for children
test: A girl in Auckland wakes to a beeping alarm and a smoky room. What should she do first? | Get low and crawl to the safest way out | Stand up tall and look for where the fire is | Open the window and wait in bed for help | Hide in the bedroom cupboard until it stops
test: Which statement is true? | Smoke collects near the ceiling first | Smoke always sinks down to the floor first | Flames kill many more people than smoke does | Fire takes hours to fill a room with smoke
test: Leo's shirt catches fire from a candle. What should he do? | Stop, drop to the ground, cover his face and roll | Run outside fast so the wind blows the flames out | Wave his arms to shake off the flames | Pull the shirt off over his head while running to the tap
test: What would most likely happen if a family had no meeting place? | Someone might go back in looking for a person who is safe | They would escape more quickly from the house | The firefighters would arrive sooner | Nothing, because a meeting place is not needed
test: A boy in Phoenix feels his bedroom door and it is hot. What should he do? | Keep it shut and use his other way out | Open it quickly and run straight through | Open it slowly and look round for the fire | Pour water on the door and then open it
test: A family in Sydney has escaped a fire. Who calls 000, and from where? | Anyone safe outside, using a phone out there | The youngest child, from inside the house | Nobody, the alarm calls the brigade by itself | The parent, after going back for the phone
test: Why should a child not try to fight a house fire with a bucket of water? | Fires grow fast and getting out quickly saves lives | Water never puts out any kind of fire | A bucket is too heavy for any child | Firefighters like to put out fires themselves and get cross if you help
test: Why is a smoke alarm better at detecting a fire than a sleeping person? | It senses smoke while people are asleep and cannot smell well | It can see flames through walls | It is warmer than a person | It hears the crackle of fire from far away
test: Which is the safest thing to do when your house fills with smoke? | Crawl low under the smoke to the exit | Walk upright to see over the smoke | Hold your breath and run through the flames | Stay still and wait for the smoke to clear
test: smoke everywhere, how do i get out | Crawl low where the air is cleaner | Stand up tall and run | Hide under the bed until it stops | Open every cupboard
test: door feels hot to the back of my hand | Don't open it, use another way out | Open it quickly | Kick it open | Wait for it to cool
test: we got out but the dog's still inside | Never go back in, tell firefighters | Run in quickly for the dog | Call the dog from the doorway | Go in with a wet towel over your face
test: my sleeve's on fire!! | Stop, drop, cover your face and roll | Run for the tap | Wave your arm about | Blow on it hard
test: house is on fire but my tablet's upstairs | Get out and stay out, never go back for things | Run up quickly and get it | Go up if the smoke is thin and you hold your breath | Send your brother up for it
test: chip pan's on fire, mum's not here!! | Get out, do not use water, call for help | Throw a jug of cold water on it fast | Carry the pan outside | Blow hard on the flames
test: Noah says u can just hold ur breath and walk through smoke. right? | No, smoke kills fast, stay low and get out another way | Yes, smoke is only a smell | Yes, if you walk quickly and keep your eyes shut tight | Yes, unless the smoke is black
test: heard a family's house burned down and the dad went back in for the dog and never came out | Real lesson: once out of a fire, never go back inside, not even for a pet | Real lesson: dogs always find their own way out | It proves house fires only happen to families with dogs | It is gossip, so going back in is fine if you are quick
test: my cousin says in a fire u should hide under the bed where the smoke can't get u | Wrong; never hide in a fire, get out low and fast, because firefighters may not find you | He is right, beds block the smoke | He is right, if you take a pillow | He is right, but only in a small bedroom with the door shut and a wet towel under it
test: everyone's saying the Lees' fire was started on purpose by their son, pass it on | Do not pass it on; it is a serious rumour that could hurt an innocent family | Pass it on, because fires do not start alone | Pass it on, so the police find out | Pass it on, and add that he was laughing
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the principal cause of death in house fires? | Most such deaths are occasioned by smoke rather than by flames | Most such deaths are occasioned by falling walls | Most such deaths are occasioned by burns from the flames | Most such deaths are occasioned by the water used to extinguish the fire
exam: It being given that a door is found to be hot to the back of the hand during a fire, select the course of action consistent with established guidance. | To make use of another means of escape | To open the door rapidly and run through | To open the door slowly to admit fresh air | To wait behind the door until it has cooled
exam: Identify the action to be taken by a person whose clothing has caught fire. | To stop, to drop to the ground, to cover the face and to roll | To run as quickly as possible to fetch water | To wave the arms so as to blow out the flames | To stand still and remove all clothing
exam: Select the response that is consistent with established guidance concerning smoke alarms. | They should be installed upon every level of a dwelling and tested monthly | They should be installed only in the kitchen | They should be tested once in every ten years | They should be disconnected whilst cooking
